  "image_name": "0f210fc7c42af165181e5e6cd5577193.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how peripheral metabolic signals regulate appetite through hypothalamic circuits. On the left, the stomach, pancreas, and white adipose tissue send hormonal cues—ghrelin, insulin, and leptin/adipokines—to the hypothalamus. The right side zooms into two connected regions, the arcuate nucleus and the paraventricular hypothalamus, where these signals act on two opposing neuron populations.\n\nOne pathway centers on AgRP neurons, shown in pink, which are stimulated by ghrelin and appear to promote feeding. These neurons release AgRP, NPY, and GABA onto downstream targets, inhibiting anorexigenic neurons in the paraventricular hypothalamus and also suppressing POMC neurons locally. AgRP is depicted acting at melanocortin receptors, countering melanocortin signaling.\n\nThe opposing pathway involves POMC neurons, shown in yellow, which are activated by leptin and insulin and project to the same paraventricular target. These neurons release α-MSH, which activates MC4R on the anorexigenic neuron and promotes satiety-related output.\n\nOverall, the image shows a balance between hunger-promoting AgRP signaling and satiety-promoting POMC signaling, with circulating hormones from the gut, pancreas, and fat tissue shifting that balance.",
